{"parentUuid":"39660cb9-ee3e-4220-a9c5-16a352d2d29c","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"48697278-3205-4eb7-8c30-6f2e049e918b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WgAbs4vbpyQutCK2DFUQUy","type":"tool_result","content":"165 PLAN.md\n3:Status: PLAN, written 2026-11-15 against `main` plus the v1.2.0-rc.1 notes, at the owner's request (\"draft the v1.3 platform track as recommended\"). Amended 2026-11-23 with Codex-derived units SB-1, MT-2 and VR-3 (and an explicit MT-1 row). It follows v1.2.1 and v1.2.2 ([`docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md`](../v1.2.1/PLAN.md), decision D12). Nothing here is a claim that Nexus Code is frontier-grade, and no unit starts without the decisions in section 7.\n20:- **Codex re-read** (2026-11-23; sparse clone of [openai/codex](https://github.com/openai/codex) at `e0a6d05`, Apache-2.0; untrusted code, nothing run; XERJ hub had no `openai/codex` corpus): OS-native sandboxing behind named **permission profiles** (workspace-write roots, broad host reads, network off or managed/transparent TCP; Linux bwrap+seccomp, macOS Seatbelt, Windows restricted token) with explicit residual documentation (e.g. ignored `node_modules` injection); Starlark **execpolicy** `prefix_rule` with load-time `match`/`not_match` examples (already the model for CS-9 `command_rules.ts`); centralized **tool registry/router/orchestrator** (spec + handler bound together; approve →\n22:- **Research notes** (2026-11-15): the Anthropic 2026 agentic coding trends report (read in full; a vendor document with no methodology, customer-reported figures) and OpenHands (documentation, not source) support three priorities here: VR-1 and VR-2 (the report's own evidence is that people delegate what is cheaply checkable, so verifiability is the delegation boundary), PA-1 (it names a view of concurrent agent sessions) and AC-1 (above). They are framing, not evidence about Nexus.\n46:| PF-3 | Transport beyond a parent's stdio: a unix domain socket (default) with a token and origin check for any localhost HTTP/WebSocket listener (reference: goose `acp/transport/auth.rs`, constant-time compare); several clients may attach and reattach | a second client attaches to a running session; a wrong token is refused; the socket is mode 0600 | M | P1 |\n47:| PF-4 | Library entry point: a `lib` entry that does not run commander on import, `exports` and `types` in `cli/package.json`, a package name decision, a documented minimal embedding example that spawns the sidecar | an example script runs a verified task through the entry point in CI | M | P1 |\n63:| VR-2 | Expose verification as an MCP tool and an ACP extension so another harness can call Nexus to check its own work (`serve-mcp` today exposes workspace tools, not verification); the caller can read the verdict but cannot change it | an external client's session gets a verdict and a receipt id; tampering with the request cannot raise the verdict | M | P1 (decision D20) |\n70:| SB-1 | Named permission profiles (`:workspace`, `:read-only`, `:network` — exact names per decision D22) that map today's bwrap/docker flags, HOME mounts and network settings without loosening gates; profiles compose with approval mode and managed policy (GV-1); `nexus doctor` and session posture print the effective profile; document residual holes (broad host reads, ignored trees) honestly. Reference: Codex permission profiles and sandbox defaults write-up. Does not replace verification-path protection, CS-9 or the VERIFIED ladder | CLI and doctor show the effective profile; a profile cannot disable verification-path protection, grant allow-always, or bypass G-3; existing sandbox tes\n117:Phase B: AC-1, HK-1, GV-1, GV-2, PF-4, PA-2, VR-2, MT-1, VR-3, SB-1.\n118:Phase C: BG-1, BG-2, HK-2, PF-3, AC-2, PA-1, GV-5, MT-2.\n121:MT-2 follows MT-1 so the registry refactor targets the split spine; SB-1 may land beside GV-1 (profiles must not outrank managed policy). VR-3 can ship as soon as sandbox denials are stable under SB-1 aliases, or earlier against today's engines if the receipt shape is profile-agnostic.\n141:| D13 | Publish an npm package or SDK (PF-4)? Package name and licence; external publishing is yours to authorise | no publish; the entry point is documented but not published |\n148:| D20 | Position Nexus as a verifier for other harnesses (VR-2)? | yes |\n150:| D22 | Permission profile names and defaults (SB-1): which named profiles ship, whether `:workspace` is the interactive default, and whether managed policy may force a stricter profile | ship `:workspace`, `:read-only`, `:network`; interactive default stays today's engine defaults until an explicit profile is set; managed policy may force a stricter profile, never a looser one |\nShell cwd was reset to /home/mike/nexus-code","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"dc7c831c-6448-47ae-b2d5-64117ccb7a70","timestamp":"2026-11-23T19:05:38.821Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"165 PLAN.md\n3:Status: PLAN, written 2026-11-15 against `main` plus the v1.2.0-rc.1 notes, at the owner's request (\"draft the v1.3 platform track as recommended\"). Amended 2026-11-23 with Codex-derived units SB-1, MT-2 and VR-3 (and an explicit MT-1 row). It follows v1.2.1 and v1.2.2 ([`docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md`](../v1.2.1/PLAN.md), decision D12). Nothing here is a claim that Nexus Code is frontier-grade, and no unit starts without the decisions in section 7.\n20:- **Codex re-read** (2026-11-23; sparse clone of [openai/codex](https://github.com/openai/codex) at `e0a6d05`, Apache-2.0; untrusted code, nothing run; XERJ hub had no `openai/codex` corpus): OS-native sandboxing behind named **permission profiles** (workspace-write roots, broad host reads, network off or managed/transparent TCP; Linux bwrap+seccomp, macOS Seatbelt, Windows restricted token) with explicit residual documentation (e.g. ignored `node_modules` injection); Starlark **execpolicy** `prefix_rule` with load-time `match`/`not_match` examples (already the model for CS-9 `command_rules.ts`); centralized **tool registry/router/orchestrator** (spec + handler bound together; approve →\n22:- **Research notes** (2026-11-15): the Anthropic 2026 agentic coding trends report (read in full; a vendor document with no methodology, customer-reported figures) and OpenHands (documentation, not source) support three priorities here: VR-1 and VR-2 (the report's own evidence is that people delegate what is cheaply checkable, so verifiability is the delegation boundary), PA-1 (it names a view of concurrent agent sessions) and AC-1 (above). They are framing, not evidence about Nexus.\n46:| PF-3 | Transport beyond a parent's stdio: a unix domain socket (default) with a token and origin check for any localhost HTTP/WebSocket listener (reference: goose `acp/transport/auth.rs`, constant-time compare); several clients may attach and reattach | a second client attaches to a running session; a wrong token is refused; the socket is mode 0600 | M | P1 |\n47:| PF-4 | Library entry point: a `lib` entry that does not run commander on import, `exports` and `types` in `cli/package.json`, a package name decision, a documented minimal embedding example that spawns the sidecar | an example script runs a verified task through the entry point in CI | M | P1 |\n63:| VR-2 | Expose verification as an MCP tool and an ACP extension so another harness can call Nexus to check its own work (`serve-mcp` today exposes workspace tools, not verification); the caller can read the verdict but cannot change it | an external client's session gets a verdict and a receipt id; tampering with the request cannot raise the verdict | M | P1 (decision D20) |\n70:| SB-1 | Named permission profiles (`:workspace`, `:read-only`, `:network` — exact names per decision D22) that map today's bwrap/docker flags, HOME mounts and network settings without loosening gates; profiles compose with approval mode and managed policy (GV-1); `nexus doctor` and session posture print the effective profile; document residual holes (broad host reads, ignored trees) honestly. Reference: Codex permission profiles and sandbox defaults write-up. Does not replace verification-path protection, CS-9 or the VERIFIED ladder | CLI and doctor show the effective profile; a profile cannot disable verification-path protection, grant allow-always, or bypass G-3; existing sandbox tes\n117:Phase B: AC-1, HK-1, GV-1, GV-2, PF-4, PA-2, VR-2, MT-1, VR-3, SB-1.\n118:Phase C: BG-1, BG-2, HK-2, PF-3, AC-2, PA-1, GV-5, MT-2.\n121:MT-2 follows MT-1 so the registry refactor targets the split spine; SB-1 may land beside GV-1 (profiles must not outrank managed policy). VR-3 can ship as soon as sandbox denials are stable under SB-1 aliases, or earlier against today's engines if the receipt shape is profile-agnostic.\n141:| D13 | Publish an npm package or SDK (PF-4)? Package name and licence; external publishing is yours to authorise | no publish; the entry point is documented but not published |\n148:| D20 | Position Nexus as a verifier for other harnesses (VR-2)? | yes |\n150:| D22 | Permission profile names and defaults (SB-1): which named profiles ship, whether `:workspace` is the interactive default, and whether managed policy may force a stricter profile | ship `:workspace`, `:read-only`, `:network`; interactive default stays today's engine defaults until an explicit profile is set; managed policy may force a stricter profile, never a looser one |","stderr":"\nShell cwd was reset to /home/mike/nexus-code","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"39660cb9-ee3e-4220-a9c5-16a352d2d29c","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
