{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-23T19:18:10.597Z","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a1a4b2d9ebf834e68</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01K762XEVvdb6WCVGaNoQPpY</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/a1a4b2d9ebf834e68.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Plan VR-2 verify for clients\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I wrote the VR-2 plan to `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/VR2_PLAN.md`. The design needs no change to `cli/src/core/verify.ts` or the verify-result schema (stays v3). Two claim conflicts, covered under Main risks, will delay slices 1 and 2.\n\n**Decisions taken**\n1. **The caller controls nothing.** The MCP tool `nexus_verify` takes no arguments, and the ACP method `_nexus/verify` takes only `sessionId`. Any extra field (a verifier, base, path, claimed outcome) is rejected with an error and no verification runs.\n2. **The base commit is pinned when the server or session starts.** Otherwise the caller could commit weakened tests first and be judged against them. The change checked is always the working tree on disk.\n3. **Only the operator's verifier runs.** That is the existing `--authoritative-verifier` flag, and `--deliverable-file` supplies the deliverables. A verifier discovered in the repo is never approved on these surfaces, so the caller gets the existing BLOCKED / `VERIFIER_NOT_APPROVED` with nothing run and is never prompted. Without deliverables the best result is REGRESSION_FREE.\n4. **Sandbox is always on, network off, no opt-out.** Running the verifier is acceptable: serve-mcp already lets the same caller run arbitrary shell commands through `run_command` without approval.\n5. **The call is refused** in read-only mode, when managed policy requires a human for command execution, while another verification runs (one per process, not queued), and in ACP while a prompt runs on that session. Prompts are also refused during a verification.\n6. **Verification runs in a forked worker process.** `verifyChange` runs its child processes synchronously (`spawnSync`), so in-process it would freeze the server for minutes. The fork also makes timeouts and cancellation enforceable.\n7. **Receipts:**\n   - ids are `vr-` plus 32 random hex digits, kept in memory per process (32 at most), with a SHA-256 of the served document.\n   - MCP reads them back through `resources/read nexus://verify-receipt/&lt;id&gt;`; ACP through `_nexus/receipt/get` by explicit id, bound to the session.\n   - Without an id, `receipt/get` still returns turn receipts only, so existing clients see no change.\n   - There is no file store: anything the server's user can write, the caller can overwrite with `run_command`.\n8. **Every verdict, BLOCKED included, is a successful result with a receipt.** An error comes back only when no verdict exists.\n\n**Slices**\n1. **Shared service and MCP tool (M):** new `cli/src/core/verify_service.ts` and `verify_worker.ts`; edits to `cli/src/mcp.ts`, `cli/src/index.ts` (serve-mcp action), one exported helper in `cli/src/config.ts`, `docs/MCP_GUIDE.md`, `docs/USER_MANUAL.md`. New tests `verify_service.test.ts` and `mcp_verify.test.ts`.\n2. **ACP extension (S/M):** `cli/src/acp_stdio.ts`, `acp/types.ts`, `acp/validate.ts`, `acp/mapping.ts`, the regenerated ACP schema and its ledger entry, `docs/ACP.md`, `cli/test/helpers/acp_examples.ts`. New test `acp_verify.test.ts`; updates to `acp_schema.test.ts` and `acp_mapping.test.ts`.\n3. **Docs and evidence (S):** new invariant row `I-AUTH-6` in `docs/INVARIANTS.md`, `docs/protocol/README.md`, `docs/evidence/VR2_VERIFY_FOR_CLIENTS.md`, a status note in `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md`.\n\nThe requirements ledger and the tool-description budget tests are unaffected: the new tool is not in Nexus's own model-facing tool list.\n\n**Main risks**\n- **Same-user tampering:** the guarantee covers the request only. A caller with shell as the server's user can still tamper with `node_modules`, `~/.gitconfig` content filters (which verify trusts as operator config) or the Nexus install. Only OS isolation stops that; the plan documents it as a limit.\n- **Claim conflicts:**\n  - `index.ts` is held by an AC-1 claim whose branch has merged, but it is under 24 hours old (until 2026-11-24T01:10Z), so only the owner can release it before then. This blocks slice 1.\n  - `acp_stdio.ts` is in open PR #754, so slice 2 starts after it merges.\n  - The ACP types, mapping and docs claims belong to a Codex process (pid 11247) that is still alive.\n- **Existing serve-mcp gaps, left out of scope:** no input line-length cap, `--read-only` ignored for workspace tools, hard-coded server version `1.2.1`.\n\n**Could not determine**\n- Whether forking a `.ts` worker inherits `--import tsx` under the test runner on Node 22 and 24. Check this first in slice 1.\n- Whether the Codex agent holding the ACP claims is still working on them.\n- XERJ was available but its corpora have no peer exposing verification as an MCP tool or ACP extension. The plan's reference-coding section records the queries and the hits from opencode.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>230979</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>78</tool_uses><duration_ms>709940</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
